<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- com.roborev.daemon.plist
     launchd supervision for the roborev review daemon (llm#936, llm#956).

     WHY THIS EXISTS
     `roborev daemon start` self-daemonizes: forks, detaches, and returns a
     shell prompt. Nothing then owns its lifecycle, nothing restarts it,
     nothing bounds its age, and it appears in no health check. That
     self-daemonized process ran for 7 days holding a stale GEMINI_API_KEY
     after a rotation; every review failed the whole time and nobody knew
     (llm#936). This plist replaces the self-daemonizing entrypoint with
     launchd supervision via roborev_daemon_launcher.sh, which re-reads
     secrets fresh on every start (RunAtLoad, and every KeepAlive restart)
     and execs `roborev daemon run` in the foreground so its PID is the one
     launchd actually supervises.

     Once installed, never run `roborev daemon start` again on this
     machine — the two would race for the same job queue. Restarts go
     through launchd: `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.roborev.daemon`.

     See .claude/rules/long-running-process-supervision.md for the general
     invariant.

     HOW THE DAEMON ACTUALLY GETS STARTED (measured 2026-08-14, corrects two
     wrong premises this header previously carried)
       Any roborev CLIENT auto-spawns a daemon when none is reachable. The
       instance running before this migration was NOT an orphan from a hand-
       typed `daemon start`: its parent was `roborev stream` (a client),
       whose parent was roborev_auto_refine.sh, whose parent was launchd via
       com.roborev.auto-refine. Whole tree started in the same second.

         4574 roborev daemon run          <- PPID 4572
         4572 roborev stream              <- PPID 4561, spawned the daemon
         4561 roborev_auto_refine.sh run  <- PPID 1 (com.roborev.auto-refine)

       Two consequences for the sequence below:
         a) Stop com.roborev.auto-refine FIRST. Killing only the daemon
            leaves its `stream` client running, which immediately spawns a
            replacement and races the launchd one.
         b) The race is bounded but not free: the daemon binds
            127.0.0.1:7373, so a second instance cannot bind and exits.
            Whoever holds the port wins; the loser dies. With KeepAlive +
            ThrottleInterval that means a client-spawned rival can hold the
            port for up to a minute while launchd's copy crash-loops.

       `roborev daemon stop` DOES exist in this version and works (verified:
       prints "Daemon stopped", exit 0). An earlier draft of this header
       claimed otherwise and told the operator to `kill` by PID.

     MIGRATION SEQUENCE (orchestrator step, after PR review)
       1. Stop the job whose client spawns the daemon, so nothing respawns
          one mid-migration:
            launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.roborev.auto-refine
          Then stop the daemon itself and confirm the port is free:
            roborev daemon stop
            pgrep -fl roborev                       # expect no output
            lsof -nP -iTCP:7373 -sTCP:LISTEN        # expect no output

       2. Install this plist:
            cp .claude/launchd/com.roborev.daemon.plist \
               ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.roborev.daemon.plist

       3. Bootstrap it:
            launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
              ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.roborev.daemon.plist

       4. Verify a NEW pid appears, that launchd's supervised pid IS the
          daemon's pid (proves the launcher exec'd rather than forked), and
          that its parent is launchd:
            launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.roborev.daemon | grep pid
            pgrep -fl "roborev daemon run"
            ps -o pid,ppid,command -p <pid>          # PPID must be 1
            lsof -nP -iTCP:7373 -sTCP:LISTEN         # must be the new pid

       5. Restore auto-refine and confirm its client CONNECTS rather than
          spawning a rival — exactly one "daemon run" must remain:
            launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) \
              ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.roborev.auto-refine.plist
            pgrep -fl roborev

       6. Verify the daemon actually processes work, not just that a PID
          exists — make one small commit in a repo with roborev's
          post-commit hook installed, then confirm a new row lands in
          ~/.roborev/reviews.db for that commit within a couple of minutes:
            sqlite3 ~/.roborev/reviews.db \
              "SELECT id, status, agent, enqueued_at FROM review_jobs \
               ORDER BY enqueued_at DESC LIMIT 3;"

       ROLLBACK (only if step 4, 5 or 6 fails)
            launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.roborev.daemon
          This returns to NO supervised daemon running — the safe failure
          mode. Note that bootstrapping com.roborev.auto-refine again is by
          itself enough to get an UNsupervised daemon back (its client
          spawns one), which is the pre-migration state; treat that as a
          temporary stopgap only, and reopen llm#956 rather than calling it
          resolved.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.roborev.daemon</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Users/johngavin/.claude/scripts/roborev_daemon_launcher.sh</string>
    </array>

    <!-- The daemon resolves review-agent binaries from PATH. Originally
         copied from com.roborev.auto-refine.plist, which only ever needed
         `gemini`/`codex` — so `claude-code` was never in scope and
         ~/.local/bin was omitted.

         Measured 2026-08-18: with the old PATH,
           env -i PATH=<old> /usr/bin/which claude codex gemini
         resolved codex and gemini but NOT claude (~/.local/bin/claude).
         The configured backup agent was therefore unreachable, and every
         gemini quota failure died with

           no review agent available: no configured agent available
           (preferred: "claude-code", backups: claude-code)

         instead of falling back to claude-code. The waterfall was
         configured correctly and had nowhere to fall. Verified after
         adding ~/.local/bin: the same forced-override review that
         previously errored returned
           Enqueued job 12545 for c228cb3 (agent: claude-code)
         and ran under claude-code. See llm#746.

         If a new review agent is added, verify it resolves from THIS PATH,
         not from an interactive shell's — `roborev check-agents` run by
         hand passes on binaries the daemon cannot see, which is exactly
         how this stayed hidden.

         /usr/bin:/bin were moved ahead of /opt/homebrew/bin on 2026-08-28
         (llm#1036 follow-up): /opt/homebrew/bin/bash is a symlink to
         Homebrew's Cellar bash, which is ad-hoc signed (empty csreq) and
         can never hold a durable TCC grant. With homebrew first in PATH,
         any child process this daemon spawns that execs a bare `bash` (not
         an absolute path) — e.g. a review agent's shell tool calls —
         resolved to that ad-hoc binary and re-prompted on every TCC-gated
         access, even after the daemon's OWN script had its shebang pinned
         to Apple-signed /bin/bash. Neither codex/gemini/claude nor roborev
         itself live under /usr/bin or /bin, so moving these two ahead does
         not change which review-agent binaries resolve — only which `bash`
         a bare invocation finds.

         /opt/homebrew/bin was REMOVED from PATH entirely on 2026-09-24
         (llm#1136): `roborev daemon run` shells out to
         `/opt/homebrew/bin/agentsview session usage <id> --format json` to
         backfill token/cost data on every review. With no agentsview
         server reachable on 127.0.0.1, that client self-spawns
         `agentsview serve`, which re-parents to PID 1 (the daemon does not
         supervise it) and was found running 66h straight at ~97% CPU;
         killing it, it respawned within ~5s on the next review. Neither
         roborev nor agentsview exposes a config switch to suppress this
         (`[cost].include_costs = false` and `[cost].endpoint = ''` were
         already set in .roborev.toml and made no difference — the client
         call happens regardless). Since agentsview lives ONLY under
         /opt/homebrew/bin and nowhere else on this machine, dropping that
         directory from the daemon's PATH makes `command -v agentsview`
         fail inside the daemon's process tree, so the shellout errors
         instead of spawning a replacement server. Re-verified every other
         tool this PATH must resolve, under the NEW PATH (env -i PATH=<new>
         /bin/sh -c 'command -v <tool>'):
           claude     -> /Users/johngavin/.local/bin/claude
           codex      -> /Users/johngavin/.nvm/versions/node/v24.13.0/bin/codex
           gemini     -> /Users/johngavin/.nvm/versions/node/v24.13.0/bin/gemini
           git        -> /usr/bin/git
           node       -> /Users/johngavin/.nvm/versions/node/v24.13.0/bin/node
           sqlite3    -> /usr/bin/sqlite3
           roborev    -> /Users/johngavin/.local/bin/roborev
           agentsview -> MISSING (intended — see above)
           gh         -> MISSING (deliberate, and fine: [ci].enabled = false
                         in .roborev.toml, and `strings` on the roborev
                         binary shows no `gh` invocation — it talks to
                         GitHub over HTTP with GH_TOKEN, not via the CLI)
         This also removes the last remaining use of /opt/homebrew/bin from
         this daemon's PATH, so the ad-hoc-signed-bash TCC exposure the
         2026-08-28 change above already warned about (bare `bash` in a
         homebrew-first PATH) no longer has a homebrew entry to resolve to
         at all — a residual hardening, not just the agentsview fix.
         Revert: re-add ":/opt/homebrew/bin" to the PATH string below if a
         future tool genuinely needs it; re-litigate the agentsview
         self-spawn problem separately rather than reflexively restoring
         the old PATH. -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/johngavin/.local/bin:/Users/johngavin/.nvm/versions/node/v24.13.0/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/local/bin</string>
        <key>GEMINI_CLI_TRUST_WORKSPACE</key>
        <string>true</string>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Perpetual process, not a scheduled task: restart on crash or
         reboot, never exit on its own. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Bound a crash-loop: if the daemon exits within 10s of starting
         (e.g. GEMINI_API_KEY genuinely absent, exit 78 from the launcher),
         wait at least this many seconds before the next KeepAlive restart
         rather than spinning. Matches roborev_bridge's precedent value. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>60</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/johngavin/.claude/logs/roborev-daemon.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/johngavin/.claude/logs/roborev-daemon.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
